+## SRE application benchmark
+
+The SRE Config2Spec F profile has been migrated to the current Java library and
+native engine, preserving its Header-Link encoding, trimming, LSB-first label
+order and borrowed integer-handle API. The native API now supports its 108-bit
+link fields. Nine fattree/MF cases were run twice per engine in fresh processes
+(54 runs); route counts, layout, ordering and RIB/FIB condition checks matched.
+
+The new `prioritySelectBatch` computes multiple independent lists in one Lace
+RUN, including ordered prefix preparation inside each list. SRE uses it after
+BGP route insertion and global-RIB installation, applying results in the original
+order. Two fresh-process runs per configuration, F all BCDD:
+
+| Case | Original Java s | New native1 s | New native6 s | Native1/native6 | Java/native6 |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| fattree08 / MF3 | 8.088 | 7.412 | 6.188 | 1.198× | 1.307× |
+| fattree12 / MF3 | 323.948 | 121.398 | 68.434 | 1.774× | 4.734× |
+
+The 12/MF3 SRC computation scales by 2.027× from one to six workers. Native
+diagram budget is 3 GiB, JVM heap 8 GiB, process guard 12 GiB, label tasks off,
+cache entries 4M/16M and explicit GC high-water mark 1228 MiB. Each configuration
+runs twice on the same six physical cores. The default adapter water mark is
+unchanged. This is end-to-end scaling; C implementation gains relative to Java
+are separate from thread scaling. The [report](results/sre_priority_batch_20261012/README.md)
+includes same-build OFF and frozen previous-native controls, memory, phases,
+output audits and reproduction. The original complete matrix remains below.
+
+The earlier deep optimization exceeds the frozen original Java control on the
+heavier cases. Two runs per configuration, F all BCDD, same input/encoding and
+12 GiB process RSS ceiling:
+
+| Case | Original Java s | Native 2 workers s | Native 6 workers s | Java/native2 |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| fattree08 / MF3 | 7.730 | 7.108 | 7.516 | 1.087× |
+| fattree12 / MF2 | 24.068 | 23.275 | 25.391 | 1.034× |
+| fattree12 / MF3 | 320.645 | 172.431 | 179.131 | 1.860× |
+
+The largest case uses 60.6% of Java's mean peak RSS with two workers, and its
+native1/native2 scaling is 1.120×. Small inputs remain slower; six workers do
+not consistently beat two. These results include algorithm and capacity tuning,
+not only adding threads. Set `sre.ndd.workers=2` and explicit cache capacities
+(04:262144, 08:4194304, 12:16777216); compatibility defaults remain 6/262144.
+All 72 fresh-process runs passed output audits, with additional four-backend,
+JNI, GC and sanitizer checks. See the [complete nine-case matrix, memory table,
+phase timings and reproduction](results/sre_deep_optimization_20261012/README.md).
+
+The following table records the initial scalar integration, before these changes.
+
+The initial scalar JNI integration **did not achieve a speedup**: six workers were
+slower than one worker in all nine cases. Native memory usage was lower on the
+larger inputs. That measured version did not use native batches, and label task
+spawning was disabled. Java and native cache capacities also differ, as recorded
+in the report; these results describe this integration and configuration.
+
+| fattree12 MF | Java seconds | native1 seconds | native6 seconds | Java RSS MiB | native6 RSS MiB |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| 1 | 6.306 | 31.485 | 40.723 | 2371.1 | 1008.6 |
+| 2 | 22.936 | 160.989 | 170.593 | 3054.3 | 1268.6 |
+| 3 | 320.625 | 575.458 | 608.544 | 6137.0 | 2233.1 |
+
+Times and RSS are arithmetic means of two independent runs. Validation for this
+migration passed native CTest at 1/2/4/6 workers, 33 NDD JUnit tests, 35 SRE JUnit
+tests, and ASan/UBSan at 1/6 workers. The Java BCDD canonicalization fix and exact
+sources are archived with the results. See the [full nine-case report and reproduction scripts](results/sre_parallel_20261011_final/README.md).
+
+Follow-up profiling found correlated native-handle hash collisions in Java and
+whole-table scratch allocation on every `satCount`. The hash is fixed; persistent
+per-node counts were tested with `PNDD_EXPERIMENT_SATCOUNT_CACHE=ON` (now default
+ON, 16 additional bytes per node on the measured platform). In the initial paired fattree08/MF3
+runs, these changes reduced native1/native6 time from 37.398/40.341 to
+23.379/24.828 seconds. Six workers still lag one worker and the 7.629-second Java
+baseline. Finer NDD tasks and label spawning did not reverse that result. See the
+[follow-up report, larger-input checks and GC experiments](results/sre_parallel_analysis_20261012/README.md).
+
+The subsequent deep optimization integrates propagation, priority-selection,
+forwarding and reachability batches into SRE. It reduces OR/DIFF residual work,
+adds a checked BCDD cofactor fast path, stack/slab storage, split and worker-local
+caches, and memory-pressure adapter collection. The original Java control is
+frozen independently of the evolving adapter. In those earlier trials, two
+workers were a useful starting point; cross-list batching now benefits from six
+on the heavier cases above. Increasing workers still does not guarantee better scaling.
+Use explicit cache capacities and consult the
+[deep optimization report and reproducible matrix](results/sre_deep_optimization_20261012/README.md)
+for gains, slower cases, memory costs and controls.
 
